;; Conformance fixture: data-classification/project-egress-explicit-nil-frame-fails-closed
;;
;; Per Spec 015 §Direct reads and fail-closed frame resolution — the `:frame`
;; opt is read by KEY PRESENCE, not truthiness. An EXPLICIT `{:frame nil}` says
;; "this value has no governing frame" and FAILS CLOSED. Only an ABSENT `:frame`
;; key falls through to the carried scope.
;;
;; This is the case the sibling `project-egress-fails-closed-no-frame.edn` does
;; NOT reach, and the distinction is the whole point of the fixture. That one
;; OMITS the key, and a Mode-B runner is expected to make an omitted `:frame`
;; genuinely frameless, so there is no frame to borrow and it would pass even
;; under a truthiness resolution. Here the key is PRESENT and nil while a LIVE
;; frame is in scope, and that frame — `:rf/default`, which this fixture gives
;; no classification at all — would walk the value to the IDENTITY and ship the
;; token RAW if the projection fell through to it. The two calls discriminate:
;;
;;   [0] `:frame nil`      ⇒ :rf/redacted        (fail closed, no borrow)
;;   [1] `:frame :rf/default` ⇒ the value VERBATIM  (that frame is live + empty)
;;
;; Call [1] is the CONTROL, and it is what makes call [0] evidence rather than a
;; restatement of the absent-key case: it establishes that the frame in scope
;; really is live and really is permissive, so a fall-through would have leaked.
;; Under the pre-rf2-kuky.5 resolution — `(or (:frame opts) (carried scope))` —
;; call [0] resolved that same live frame and returned the token verbatim.
;;
;; A port that resolves `:frame` by truthiness fails call [0] and passes call
;; [1], which is the signature to look for.
;;
;; Three consumers each minted a fresh host object as a fake frame id purely to
;; force this arm, because the request was otherwise unsayable (Xray's
;; `egress/no-frame`, Xray's local-render `fresh-dead-frame-sentinel`, and
;; core's `derivation.egress/dead-frame-sentinel`). All three are deleted; this
;; fixture is the contract that replaces them.
;;
;; Mirrors the unit-level `re-frame.elision-test/explicit-nil-frame-fails-closed-
;; under-a-live-ambient-frame` and `re-frame.projection-cljs-test/explicit-nil-
;; frame-opt-wins-over-the-ambient-scope` deftests, lifted to the host-agnostic
;; corpus.

{:fixture/id           :data-classification/project-egress-explicit-nil-frame-fails-closed
 :fixture/spec-version "1.0"
 :fixture/capabilities #{:data-classification/classification-effects}
 :fixture/doc          "project-egress with an EXPLICIT :frame nil fails closed to :rf/redacted rather than borrowing the live frame in scope, which the control call shows would have shipped the value verbatim. The opt is read by key presence, not truthiness."

 :fixture/calls
 ;; [0] The explicit nil, with a live frame in scope.
 [{:call              :project-egress
   :rf.egress/profile :rf.egress/off-box-observability
   :frame             nil
   :value             {:auth {:token "super-secret"} :public {:count 3}}
   :expect            :rf/redacted}

  ;; [1] CONTROL — the SAME value under the SAME frame, named explicitly. Its
  ;; declaration registry is empty, so the walk is the identity and the token
  ;; rides through. A green here is what makes [0]'s redaction a refusal to
  ;; borrow rather than an absence of anything to borrow.
  {:call              :project-egress
   :rf.egress/profile :rf.egress/off-box-observability
   :frame             :rf/default
   :value             {:auth {:token "super-secret"} :public {:count 3}}
   :expect            {:auth {:token "super-secret"} :public {:count 3}}}]}
